{"id": "f882f36b-c49c-456c-914f-5e27edf08dbd", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Here's my read on this policy — and I'd pump the brakes before attaching it.

**Verdict: Needs fixing first.** The combination of `iam:PassRole` + `lambda:CreateFunction` + `lambda:InvokeFunction`, all scoped to `Resource: "*"`, is a well-documented privilege-escalation path, not just a "broad permissions" smell.

| Finding | Severity | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `iam:PassRole` on `Resource: "*"` combined with `lambda:CreateFunction` + `lambda:InvokeFunction` | **Critical** | A principal with this combo can create a new Lambda function, attach **any** IAM role in the account to it (including an admin role), then invoke it — running arbitrary code with that role's permissions. This is one of the classic IAM privesc chains; it doesn't require any other misconfiguration to exploit, just this one statement. |
| `lambda:InvokeFunction` on `Resource: "*"` | High | Lets the build role invoke *any* existing function in the account, not just ones it creates — including functions that may run with elevated roles. |
| `s3:GetObject` on `Resource: "*"` | Medium | Grants read access to every object in every bucket in the account — far more than a build role typically needs, and no condition restricting source network. |
| No `Condition` blocks anywhere | Suggestion | `iam:PassRole` has no `iam:PassedToService` condition, and `s3:GetObject` has no source restriction — both are easy, high-value additions. |

**Recommended fix** — scope each action down to only what the build role actually touches:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "iam:PassRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/<lambda-execution-role-name>",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "iam:PassedToService": "lambda.amazonaws.com" }
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lambda:CreateFunction", "lambda:InvokeFunction"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:lambda:<region>:<account-id>:function:<build-prefix>-*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::<build-artifact-bucket>/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

That restricts `PassRole` to only the specific execution role(s) this pipeline is meant to hand to Lambda, scopes the Lambda actions to functions this build role actually owns, and limits S3 reads to the artifact bucket.

**Coverage note:** I tried to run this through IAM Access Analyzer's policy validator for a second syntax/security pass, but the agent's own role doesn't have `access-analyzer:ValidatePolicy` permission in this account, so that check is unavailable — the findings above are from manual review (JSON parses fine), not an automated validator run.

Want me to check what would actually break if we tightened the `iam:PassRole` resource down to specific role ARNs — i.e., whether anything else in the account currently relies on this build role passing an arbitrary role?", "type": "text"}], "created_at": null}

